            <ArticleTitle>Back and thigh pain of unusual etiology complicates rehabilitation after bilateral total knee arthroplasty.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>585-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/PHM.0b013e31817c19e7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Total knee arthroplasty is frequently performed and usually associated with favorable results. A case is presented where persistent back and thigh pain limited rehabilitation progress following bilateral total knee arthroplasty procedures. The pain was found to be related to the presence of a symptomatic 5.24-cm infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm with the pain resolving after endovascular repair of the aneurysm.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
